WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:03.003
Where are we, Joshua?

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:04.754
We're in Hannah Hall's houseboat

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:06.256
in Sausalito, California.

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:07.257
San Francisco is a bit of our

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:08.341
happy place, right? 
- It is. 

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.342
We love it up there.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.886
People keep asking me now
if I could live on a houseboat.

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.471
- No. 
- I couldn't either!

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:14.931
No, no, I don't think you could.

00:00:14.931 --> 00:00:16.558
Oh, I couldn't. Yeah.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.691
When writing this book,
I had a draft that looked

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:27.068
very unlike the story
we now know today.

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:28.319
And I remember, you

00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:29.654
came into the room
and you said,

00:00:29.654 --> 00:00:32.032
“What if you think about it
a little bit differently?”

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:35.410
When you said that, it helped me
imagine a different...

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.203
- Ending. 
- Which is a prime example

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.539
sort of, of how our writing life
works at home.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.458
We read everything
the other writes.

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:43.084
If I had my way, we 
would sit in the

00:00:43.084 --> 00:00:45.086
same office all day. 
- We do a lot.

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:46.504
You try to go to
your secret office.

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:49.215
It’s supposed to be secret
from our son, who found it.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.842
He loves it, loves it. Yeah.

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:53.470
Good day at work?

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.013
Better now that it's over.

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:58.391
It felt very natural for us to
start writing the show together.

00:00:58.391 --> 00:00:59.726
And you love being on set.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.978
I continue to learn
so much from you.

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:04.647
I'm used to adapting
historical pieces.

00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:06.733
- This is quite a departure for you. 
- It's quite a departure.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:07.734
Roll sound!

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:11.196
Adapting a historical 
event is hard

00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:12.864
‘cause there's a ton of 
research, whereas here

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:15.492
we had this great book,
and that was the huge help.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.202
What I love about 
your writing is,

00:01:17.202 --> 00:01:19.079
it's so insightful and thoughtful.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:20.622
And so, all those episodes

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:21.915
you took the first pass on

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.875
and then I just cleaned up
around the margins.

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:25.043
You can do all sorts of things

00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:26.961
in screenwriting
that I could never do though.

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.255
Making this world visual and..

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:32.425
- Dual dialogue. Using Final Draft. 
- All the things.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.593
In some ways
it felt like I was going to

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.261
film school working with you.

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:37.347
There’s gonna come a time 
when I won't be able to

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:38.473
keep her safe anymore.

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:40.517
Not from anything.

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:43.061
One of the things I’ve always loved
about the book, is that it is

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:46.106
both this suspense-mystery,

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:50.235
but it is also a meditation
on parenting and found families.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:51.486
Do you think you could 
have written that

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:53.196
before we had Jacob?
- I don't know.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.406
This book is a love
letter to Jacob.

00:01:55.406 --> 00:01:57.617
I always tell him it's 
his book, which 

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:58.952
he thinks the whole 
book’s about him.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.411
But in some ways it is.

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:03.414
You are absolutely going to be
what's best for her.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:06.209
And you know, me.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.504
It's been very nice to walk down
memory lane with you today.

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:12.257
I've adapted a bunch of books,
but I’ve never gotten to adapt

00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:15.635
a book written by my wife Laura,
which has been a joy.

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:18.012
And I only hope I 
have done it justice.

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:19.681
You did.

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:21.599
There's not a whole lot
you could tell me about

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:23.810
that would change 
anything between us.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.395
Well, thank God for that.

